{"results":[{"url":"https://factcheck.afp.com/doc.afp.com.D4YN3T6","final_url":"https://factcheck.afp.com/doc.afp.com.D4YN3T6","title":"Supreme Court has not invalidated state laws on ballots received after Election Day","description":"Millions of Americans vote by mail, but some social media users are claiming ahead of the 2026 US midterm elections that the Supreme Court has indicated it will invalidate state laws permitting ballots received after November 3 to be counted. This is false; while states have different deadlines for when votes can arrive, the nation's highest court upheld their right in June to allow counting of ballot packages postmarked by Election Day.","language":"en","text":"US elections 2026\n\nUS politics\n\nA view of the US Supreme Court in Washington, DC, on June 25, 2026 (AFP / SAUL LOEB)\n\n# Supreme Court has not invalidated state laws on ballots received after Election Day\n\n* Published on November 2, 2026 at 21:05\n* 3 min read\n* By\n  Gwen Roley,\n  AFP USA\n\nMillions of Americans vote by mail, but some social media users are claiming ahead of the 2026 US midterm elections that the Supreme Court has indicated it will invalidate state laws permitting ballots received after November 3 to be counted. This is false; while states have different deadlines for when votes can arrive, the nation's highest court upheld their right in June to allow counting of ballot packages postmarked by Election Day.\n\nxml version=\"1.0\" standalone=\"yes\"?\n\n\n\"BREAKING: In a major development, the U.S. Supreme Court has indicated it will invalidate state laws that allow ballots to be counted after Election Day,\" says an October 30 Facebook post from conservative commentator David J Harris Jr, whom AFP has repeatedly fact-checked for spreading misinformation.\n\nThe post includes a photo of Justice Samuel Alito, who Harris claimed penned the alleged decision.\n\n\"Justice Alito pointed out that it's called 'Election DAY' for a reason,\" Harris said.\n\nThe same claim, often implicating the conservative Alito, spread across Facebook, X, Instagram, Threads and TikTok ahead of the pivotal midterm elections that could change the balance of power in Washington.\n\nImage\n\nScreenshot of a Facebook post taken October 30, 2026\n\nRepublican President Donald Trump has long claimed, without evidence, that voting by mail is vulnerable to fraud.\n\nIn March, he signed an executive order aimed at restricting the practice, which would have required states to upload voter information to a United States Postal Service (USPS) database (archived here and here).\n\nDemocratic-led states and voting rights organizations sued over the proposed changes on grounds that the US Constitution grants states -- not the federal government -- broad control over the administration of elections (archived here, here and here).\n\nThe same principle was examined in a June Supreme Court ruling on mail-in ballot deadlines.\n\nBut contrary to the claims online, the court's decision actually upheld states' right to count ballots received after Election Day (archived here).\n\nThe case began in 2024, when the Republican National Committee and Mississippi Republican Party filed suit against Mississippi Secretary of State Michael Watson, also a Republican (archived here). Their challenge contested the southern state's law allowing ballots postmarked by Election Day to be counted for the following five business days (archived here).\n\nThe complaint wound its way through the courts before the Supreme Court ruled 5-4 in favor of Watson in June 2026.\n\n\"The Supreme Court in the Watson case held that congressional statutes setting the date for congressional elections set only the date by which voters must vote, not the day by which states must receive voters' ballots,\" said Rick Hasen, a top election law expert at the University of California, Los Angeles, in an October 30 email to AFP (archived here).\n\nAlito wrote the non-binding dissenting opinion, arguing that all votes should be received by Election Day to fit within the federal definition of the date.\n\nAFP reached out to the Supreme Court for comment, but no response was immediately forthcoming.\n\nHowever, as of November 2, there are no cases or publications mentioning mail-in voting on the court's website, after the Mississippi case and Trump's unresolved gambit over voter data (archived here).\n\nThe rules on when mail-in ballots must be received vary between states, according to the National Conference of State Legislatures (archived here). Fifteen states allow ballots to be counted if they were received after Election Day, so long as they are postmarked on or before that date.\n\nMississippi limits who qualifies to vote by mail and reported on October 26 that it had already received 56,049 of 64,031 requested mail-in ballots ahead of Election Day (archived here and here).\n\nOn its website, the USPS recommends that voters mail their ballot one week prior to their state's deadline (archived here). To ensure mail is postmarked on a particular date, voters can \"ask a retail associate at a Post Office retail counter to hand-cancel it for free.\"\n\nThe secretary of state's office in Washington state, where elections are conducted entirely by mail and postmarked ballots are accepted after Election Day, previously told AFP that those who must mail their ballots rather than using drop boxes are recommended to do so as early as possible.\n\nOn October 24, Oregon's Secretary of State recommended that people voting by mail less than a week out from Election Day head to a post office to ask for a hand postmark to ensure their ballots would be accepted (archived here).\n\nAFP has debunked other misinformation about the US midterm elections here.\n\n**Copyright © AFP 2017-2026.** Any commercial use of this content requires a subscription. Click here to find out more.\n\nIs there content that you would like AFP to fact-check? Get in touch.\n\nContact us\n\n* E-mail\n\nNext article\n\nPrevious article","author":"AFP","published_date":"2026-11-02 21:05","latency_ms":1167.8681929997765,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}